The smallest direct migration is confined to one production site caller and its local tests. The current SDK does not define or export `localDateEndpointsUtc`, so there is no SDK caller or existing public SDK return type to migrate.

| Source | Current contract and smallest required change |
| --- | --- |
| `src/islands/ChartCalculator.tsx`, unknown-time branch | Assumes two valid ordered instants, computes bodies twice, commits Moon alternatives and a singular Sun Registry record. Narrow an explicit date outcome before sampling or committing a result. An empty date must not become ordinary Moon ambiguity. |
| `src/lib/chart-date-certainty.test.ts` | Four direct helper calls: two interval endpoint assertions, two actual Sun certainty cases. Narrow the nonempty outcome; retain current ordinary/DST assertions and add empty-date and ordering expectations supplied by the interval review. |
| `src/lib/moon-certainty.test.tsx`, `unknownTimeChart` helper | Builds every downstream uncertainty fixture from two unconditionally accessible endpoints. Require nonempty range in this positive fixture; add a separate empty-outcome caller control instead of fabricating endpoint arrays. |
| `src/islands/ChartCalculator.registry-bridge.test.ts` | Names the helper in a source-string test, but does not execute the empty branch. Keep its ordering/placement checks and add meaningful behavioral coverage of no certainty/result commit when the date is empty. |
| SDK `packages/engine/src/geo.ts` | Exports offset, local-time and birth resolution only. Preserve the local-time gap policy; a civil-date-set API, if later added, is a distinct new API. |

An explicit tagged union is the smallest safe caller surface only if the interval construction proves that every supported nonempty date has one contiguous interval. For example, keep the existing `start`/`end` sampling semantics in a nonempty branch and provide an empty branch without those fields. If the mathematical result can be disjoint, expose the complete ordered interval set or an explicit unsupported outcome; a min/max envelope alone must not silently replace the set. Invalid date/zone or unresolved coverage must remain distinct from a successfully proven empty date.

Current `end` is the final included millisecond, despite an ambiguous comment referring to end-exclusion. If the new API instead returns an exclusive boundary, callers must sample `endExclusive - 1 ms`, after checking a nonempty interval. Sampling exactly `endExclusive` can introduce the following date's sign. Preserve chronological ordering, including Pisces→Aries ordering; do not alphabetize Moon alternatives. Do not turn a reversed interval into a nonempty interval by sorting its ends.

For the chart caller, resolve/narrow the date outcome before natal/receipt calculation where practical. On an empty date, return a fixed localized date-unavailable error without sampling bodies, committing a new chart/receipt, constructing a share artifact, or setting a Registry record. Keep current run/access-generation cancellation guards. The existing run start already clears receipt admission, pending sharing and Registry state, but it does not remove the previous chart, and the catch only sets an error. Explicitly decide and verify how the old chart remains identified or is cleared; never attach the new impossible date to the old result. No stored profile or immutable receipt schema needs to change for this branch.

Using `moonSignCandidates=[]` as the only empty-date handling is inadequate. `moonIsUncertain` returns true, `moonLabel` says “Needs a birth time”, and the unknown-time chart notice then claims the Moon changed signs that day and suggests two neighbors. An empty local date does not support those claims, and finding a time cannot repair a date that never occurred in that zone. This would require a separate presentation reason if the product intentionally retained a shifted-noon reference chart; early date rejection is the smaller safe migration.

Endpoint equality is not the same as complete-date certainty. `stableBodySignSlug` has a general `BodyName` input and claims proof, but only compares the first and last signs; a path can leave a sign and return between samples. Its only production invocation currently uses Sun. `moonCandidatesFromEndpoints` returns only two endpoint signs. `moonCandidates` rejects any list longer than two, and `Chart.moonSignCandidates` documents exactly one settled sign or two alternatives. Therefore, a longer/repeated/disjoint date representation cannot automatically retain a claim of all possible Moon signs. The date-range work should state the duration/motion assumptions for Sun and Moon, preserve fail-closed uncertainty where those assumptions are unsupported, and avoid silently dropping intermediate candidates. This is a source-contract observation, not a claim that this subtask independently found a particular three-sign astronomical date.

Two adjacent areas are not direct callers:

- `MoonPhaseTool` independently resolves local `00:00` and `23:59` (or explicit UTC dates) and compares Moon signs for unknown time. It misses the final minute and inherits empty/midnight-crossing concerns. Reusing a corrected date outcome there is a separate small caller improvement; a direct helper return-type change alone will not fix it. The no-city branch means UTC and must preserve that convention. Its UI also limits dates to 1800–2199.
- Daily Sky facts, validation, feed and generation use explicit UTC editions: body snapshot at 12:00 UTC, feed publication at 00:00 UTC, UTC catalog boundaries. They do not call this helper and do not need local empty-date migration. Changing those edition dates into browser-local dates would break their existing contract.

Relevant verification after a real migration: ordinary and DST positive fixtures unchanged; empty outcome performs zero endpoint body calls and makes no new chart/receipt/share/Registry commitment; previous-result behavior is explicit; unavailable-date copy does not claim Moon sign change or missing birth time; invalid/unsupported resolution is distinguished from empty; exclusive/inclusive boundary semantics tested at the final millisecond; year-0000 rollover tested only as a library contract while shared-chart/UI 1800–2199 bounds remain unchanged; downstream unknown-time Moon/share/scene tests still pass. Longer or disjoint date certainty needs its own mathematical/numerical evidence. This subtask deliberately does not implement or run that interval construction, which the parent reviewer owns.

